

-> @Component – What & Why?

   @Component tells Spring:
   “This class is a Spring-managed bean.”

   @Component
   public class Employee {
   }

Spring automatically:
    creates object
    manages lifecycle
    injects dependencies


 -> @Component vs @Service vs @Repository vs @Controller

    | Annotation  | Used For               |
    | ----------- | ---------------------- |
    | @Component  | Generic class          |
    | @Service    | Business logic layer   |
    | @Repository | DAO / database layer   |
    | @Controller | Web layer (Spring MVC) |

Example:-
    @Service
    public class EmployeeService {
    }


-> Component Scanning & @ComponentScan
    Spring must scan packages to find annotated classes.

    @ComponentScan("com.example")

    Without this  Spring won’t detect beans.


-> @Autowired – Dependency Injection

   Spring automatically injects required dependency.

   Example:-
    @Component
    public class EmployeeService {

        @Autowired
        private Employee employee;
    }
Spring finds Employee bean and injects it.


-> @Qualifier & @Primary

    Problem: Multiple Beans

        @Component
        class Car { }

        @Component
        class Bike { }

    Solution 1️⃣ @Qualifier
        @Autowired
        @Qualifier("bike")
        private Vehicle vehicle;

    Solution 2️⃣ @Primary
        @Component
        @Primary
        class Car { }
    Spring picks Car by default.

-> @Value – Inject Primitive Values

    @Value("Manprit")
    private String name;

    @Value("25")
    private int age;
